WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:04.570
Would you buy clothes that don't exist?

00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:07.298
And how much would you be willing to pay?

00:00:07.298 --> 00:00:08.610
Earlier this year,

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:11.690
Benoit Pagotto's design studio, RTFKT,

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:14.270
worked with teenage
digital artist, FEWOCiOUS,

00:00:14.270 --> 00:00:17.840
to sell 620 pairs of virtual sneakers,

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:20.512
generating a total of $3.1 million

00:00:20.512 --> 00:00:23.400
in less than five minutes.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:24.641
I'm not looking

00:00:24.641 --> 00:00:25.916
at the fashion history to be inspired.

00:00:25.916 --> 00:00:29.260
We're looking at gaming,
anime, sci-fi movies,

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:31.320
that's our reference points.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.558
Entrepreneurs in this
space say that one day

00:00:33.558 --> 00:00:36.643
virtual fashion will become ubiquitous.

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:40.500
The first step is
understanding how the blurring

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:44.290
of physical and digital worlds
will change our definition

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:45.520
of what's real.

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:47.703
We have one consciousness, right?

00:00:47.703 --> 00:00:51.336
I can read Twitter and get pissed off.

00:00:51.336 --> 00:00:53.270
I don't get digital pissed off.

00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:56.330
We kinda need to let go
of this notion of kind of,

00:00:56.330 --> 00:00:59.280
oh, that's digital, this is physical.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:00.960
We've all been increasingly living

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.440
in something called the metaverse.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:05.537
The metaverse is nothing

00:01:05.537 --> 00:01:07.250
but the internet breaking, kind of free,

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:09.150
from that two-dimensional barrier

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:10.820
and into this
three-dimensional environment.

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:13.849
And then we're gonna start
exploring more and more

00:01:13.849 --> 00:01:16.000
with our daily life and our
daily life becomes enhanced.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.249
Then that's what we see as the metaverse.

00:01:18.249 --> 00:01:21.990
If it sounds a bit like
science fiction to you,

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:24.260
then you're on the right track.

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:27.380
The de-materialization of fashion is here

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:31.173
and it's going to change the
fashion business as we know it.

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:36.810
What we wear says a lot about who we are.

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:41.240
Yet fashion is also a $2.5
trillion global industry

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:43.453
that touches everyone on Earth.

00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:46.380
I'm Imran Amed.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:48.020
I first started trying to make sense

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:50.510
of the fashion business 15 years ago

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:54.350
as it was being transformed
by technology, globalization,

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:56.503
and shifting consumer values.

00:01:57.890 --> 00:01:59.220
Now I'm on a journey to see

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:01.911
how fashion is recalibrating
amid the pandemic,

00:02:01.911 --> 00:02:05.510
to balance profit with purpose.

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:07.690
This is The Business of Fashion Show.

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:10.370
Join me to see how
fashion shapes business,

00:02:10.370 --> 00:02:12.428
culture, and identity,

00:02:12.428 --> 00:02:15.873
and to meet the people
forging fashion's future.

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:24.040
As the world stayed home
during the pandemic,

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:26.803
there was still somewhere
to go to get dressed up.

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:30.203
Welcome to the metaverse.

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:34.060
The term may come from science fiction,

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:35.640
but the business opportunities

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:37.970
in this fast evolving convergence

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:40.993
of digital and physical worlds are real.

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:44.270
The metaverse has grown out of gaming,

00:02:44.270 --> 00:02:48.100
where players already spend
over 100 billion dollars

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:50.343
a year on virtual goods.

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:54.070
3 billion people,

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:56.790
more than a third of
the world's population,

00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:59.620
regularly play video games.

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:02.480
46% of gamers are women,

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:05.520
and across generations
people prefer video games

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:07.800
over movies and TV shows,

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:11.643
making gaming the world's
leading form of entertainment.

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:15.330
Dressing avatars has come a long way

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:17.310
from battleground beginnings.

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:20.560
As gaming emerges as a lucrative
channel for luxury brands

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:22.640
to reach new customers,

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:25.493
screen wear is the new street wear.

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:29.810
A growing trend in this new fashion space?

00:03:29.810 --> 00:03:33.620
Matching real-life purchases
with a digital twin.

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:35.960
New breed creatives make playful tributes

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:38.120
to their favorite brands.

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:41.810
Real-world cities embrace
virtual fashion shows

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:44.210
and virtual department stores.

00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:46.240
So don't write this off as a blip.

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:47.913
It's a game changer,

00:03:47.913 --> 00:03:51.093
and we cannot yet know where it might go.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.690
Demand for one-off NFTs, unique
certified digital assets,

00:03:58.690 --> 00:03:59.683
is soaring.

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:04.330
In fashion, NFTs are the
Couture of the metaverse,

00:04:04.330 --> 00:04:07.550
where the unfettered
creations of digital artisans

00:04:07.550 --> 00:04:10.150
can out price those of global brands,

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:12.750
and hyped assets, driven by scarcity,

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:14.940
are crypto status symbols.

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:16.310
The upside?

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:18.810
Measurable planetary savings.

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:23.140
Production of a digital
garment emits 97% less CO2

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:25.000
than a physical one.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:26.510
The downside?

00:04:26.510 --> 00:04:28.720
Minting and mining NFTs requires

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:32.550
skyrocketing energy
consumption in the real world.

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:35.410
The shift to more
environmentally efficient methods

00:04:35.410 --> 00:04:36.963
can't come fast enough.

00:04:38.390 --> 00:04:41.620
Yet virtual fashion
facilitates the expression

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:45.670
of multiple selves in a virtual
world where brands recreate

00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:49.570
the experiences that
define fashion culture,

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:52.130
while not forgetting our eternal joy

00:04:52.130 --> 00:04:54.563
of getting dressed up in real life.

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:05.970
Paris is the global capital of fashion,

00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:08.160
and virtual fashion is no exception

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:09.760
as the city sets its sights

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:12.123
on becoming more technology-focused.

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:16.170
My first stop is to meet with Ian Rogers,

00:05:16.170 --> 00:05:19.700
who moved to Paris from
Silicon Valley in 2015,

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:23.863
when he was appointed Chief
Digital Officer of LVMH,

00:05:23.863 --> 00:05:27.788
acting as a kind of digital
whisperer to C-suite executives

00:05:27.788 --> 00:05:29.778
of more than 75 luxury brands.

00:05:31.314 --> 00:05:32.147
Hey Imran.

00:05:32.147 --> 00:05:32.980
Hey!

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:33.954
Welcome! Bienvenue.

00:05:33.954 --> 00:05:34.810
Merci!

00:05:34.810 --> 00:05:38.440
Now, Ian is Chief Experience
Officer of Ledger,

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:40.939
whose security technology
helps customers protect

00:05:40.939 --> 00:05:45.280
their crypto assets using
bespoke hardware and software.

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:47.800
Ian is the perfect person to explain,

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:50.880
what exactly is an NFT anyway?

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:52.440
So I was a part of a company

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:54.250
in the late '90s called Nullsoft

00:05:54.250 --> 00:05:56.210
that made an MP3 player called Winamp.

00:05:56.210 --> 00:05:59.840
But this is actually the
image of an NFT that I bought

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:02.520
on the website Foundation.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:04.800
On the blockchain, the
art only has one owner.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:07.250
That's why it's a non-fungible token.

00:06:07.250 --> 00:06:08.083
Correct.

00:06:08.083 --> 00:06:09.610
So if you have a photograph series

00:06:09.610 --> 00:06:10.850
where there's one, two, three, four, five,

00:06:10.850 --> 00:06:12.410
six, seven, eight, nine, 10,

00:06:12.410 --> 00:06:15.057
it matters if you have
one of 10 or two of 10.

00:06:15.057 --> 00:06:17.056
On the other hand, if I owe you $20,

00:06:17.056 --> 00:06:19.330
you don't care which $20 I give you.

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:20.163
Right.

00:06:20.163 --> 00:06:21.550
You just want $20 worth of value. Right?

00:06:21.550 --> 00:06:25.820
So non-fungible means
it's gotta be the one.

00:06:25.820 --> 00:06:30.430
So this is a one-of-one
of a Winamp tribute art,

00:06:30.430 --> 00:06:33.531
which, yes, is displayed on
a screen in my living room.

00:06:33.531 --> 00:06:35.150
And how much did it cost?

00:06:35.150 --> 00:06:39.768
I spent 1.137 ETH on it.

00:06:39.768 --> 00:06:42.370
ETH is a digital currency.

00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:44.160
Correct, so that's Ethereum.

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:45.660
Ian is uniquely positioned

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:47.810
to give us foundational knowledge

00:06:47.810 --> 00:06:50.000
about the opportunities created

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:54.323
as crypto technologies,
gaming, and fashion converge.

00:06:56.340 --> 00:06:58.846
Fashion plays such a big role

00:06:58.846 --> 00:07:03.220
in helping people express their identity.

00:07:03.220 --> 00:07:05.600
And if lots of people are starting

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:09.419
to spend a lot of time in virtual spaces.

00:07:09.419 --> 00:07:10.252
Yep.

00:07:10.252 --> 00:07:11.500
It seems natural, then,

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:14.980
that they could use fashion as a vehicle

00:07:14.980 --> 00:07:18.560
for expressing their
identity in those spaces.

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:19.393
Does that make sense to you?

00:07:19.393 --> 00:07:21.682
I think 100%. I think it's inevitable.

00:07:21.682 --> 00:07:24.066
I think that this is a generational shift

00:07:24.066 --> 00:07:26.282
and I look at my 14-year-old,

00:07:26.282 --> 00:07:27.690
and she has spent

00:07:27.690 --> 00:07:29.800
the last year and a half
living in a metaverse.

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:32.180
She doesn't even know that word, right?

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:34.330
But her school is on Zoom.

00:07:34.330 --> 00:07:36.500
She hangs out with her friends online

00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:37.420
in one form or another,

00:07:37.420 --> 00:07:39.250
actually in a whole bunch
of different forms, right?

00:07:39.250 --> 00:07:42.880
In Instagram, in TikTok,
in iMessage, in Fortnite,

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:47.030
in Animal Crossing, in, you know, well,

00:07:47.030 --> 00:07:49.560
all of these different, you
know, metaverses, right?

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:53.340
And so for them, having
a digital collection,

00:07:53.340 --> 00:07:55.070
it's completely natural.

00:07:55.070 --> 00:07:58.260
Look what happened to us even,
you know, with photographs.

00:07:58.260 --> 00:08:00.850
We used to take photographs and, you know,

00:08:00.850 --> 00:08:03.470
have them developed and
hang them up in our homes.

00:08:03.470 --> 00:08:05.330
And there were many
companies that thought,

00:08:05.330 --> 00:08:08.520
oh, this digital camera thing,
like who wants that, right?

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:11.770
Most kids don't even have
physical photos, right?

00:08:11.770 --> 00:08:14.590
They only have collections
of digital photos.

00:08:14.590 --> 00:08:17.190
And so I think that you
could expect the same thing

00:08:17.190 --> 00:08:18.500
with many physical things.

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:21.846
Why would I want a collection
of stuff that no one can see,

00:08:21.846 --> 00:08:23.380
when I can have a collection

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:25.110
of digital stuff that everyone can see?

00:08:25.110 --> 00:08:26.950
I think, for our younger generation,

00:08:26.950 --> 00:08:29.920
this is just going to become
kind of natural and obvious.

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:31.250
Do you see, you know,

00:08:31.250 --> 00:08:35.630
big luxury fashion brands,
and fashion brands generally,

00:08:35.630 --> 00:08:38.210
do you see them in the foreseeable future

00:08:38.210 --> 00:08:39.470
getting into the place

00:08:39.470 --> 00:08:41.680
where they're just selling virtual goods?

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:43.190
I think they will, but to be honest,

00:08:43.190 --> 00:08:44.370
I think it's a mistake.

00:08:44.370 --> 00:08:46.610
I would wait longer if I were them.

00:08:46.610 --> 00:08:47.970
I think it's inevitable,

00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:49.100
but I think that, you know,

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:51.030
the reason that NFTs have
taken off at the beginning

00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:53.920
of this year is because we
have these NFT marketplaces.

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:57.370
You know, if you don't have a
place to display these things,

00:08:57.370 --> 00:08:59.230
then it doesn't exist.

00:08:59.230 --> 00:09:00.370
So I think that the progression

00:09:00.370 --> 00:09:03.550
is more going to be that we start to get,

00:09:03.550 --> 00:09:04.710
that's why I say I'm excited

00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:06.510
about the things that happen at scale,

00:09:06.510 --> 00:09:07.900
where we start to get, you know,

00:09:07.900 --> 00:09:11.942
a collection of digital goods
with our physical goods,

00:09:11.942 --> 00:09:15.391
we then start to take those
into our metaverse worlds.

00:09:15.391 --> 00:09:19.080
So I think that once I can
take that digital shoe,

00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:21.843
and it changes my character in Fortnite,

00:09:21.843 --> 00:09:24.380
ah, now the dominoes start to fall.

00:09:24.380 --> 00:09:28.180
And now you can sell me a
digital good only, right?

00:09:28.180 --> 00:09:29.630
But you need that second piece.

00:09:29.630 --> 00:09:33.100
You need the place for it
to live in the digital world

00:09:33.100 --> 00:09:36.490
before it's really exciting
to sell a virtual piece.

00:09:36.490 --> 00:09:38.060
So I think that, if I'm a brand,

00:09:38.060 --> 00:09:40.840
there's only downside in selling
digital pieces right now,

00:09:40.840 --> 00:09:43.150
because either it doesn't
sell for enough money,

00:09:43.150 --> 00:09:44.350
and that's a bad story,

00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:45.470
or it sells for too much money

00:09:45.470 --> 00:09:46.770
and then it's worth less next year,

00:09:46.770 --> 00:09:48.120
and that's a bad story.

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:51.140
Where if I just work on getting digital

00:09:51.140 --> 00:09:53.180
with physical at scale,

00:09:53.180 --> 00:09:55.860
the other side of that
marketplace will show up,

00:09:55.860 --> 00:09:57.960
and now digital goods will make sense.

00:09:57.960 --> 00:10:01.300
What is the underlying instinct

00:10:01.300 --> 00:10:04.380
or emotion that leads someone

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:08.140
to value something that they can't touch?

00:10:08.140 --> 00:10:10.940
As human beings we have some innate desire

00:10:10.940 --> 00:10:12.900
to sort of prove we existed,

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:15.358
and those things really
happened to us, you know?

00:10:15.358 --> 00:10:19.541
I think that the digital
collectors today, it's so early,

00:10:19.541 --> 00:10:21.790
they're speculators, you know?

00:10:21.790 --> 00:10:24.117
But there are, you know, like an art,

00:10:24.117 --> 00:10:27.830
there are people who see
something before anyone else.

00:10:27.830 --> 00:10:31.640
And I think that any time
that you give creative people,

00:10:31.640 --> 00:10:34.229
when you give a creative
community a canvas,

00:10:34.229 --> 00:10:36.879
it's just a matter of time
until they blow your mind.

00:10:39.340 --> 00:10:41.663
But how will we buy virtual fashion?

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:44.370
To understand that question,

00:10:44.370 --> 00:10:48.040
I consult with the co-founders
of THE DEMATERIALISED,

00:10:48.040 --> 00:10:50.543
a Berlin-based digital fashion startup.

00:10:51.390 --> 00:10:52.880
They explain how the lines

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:56.573
between physical and digital
realities are blurring.

00:10:57.810 --> 00:11:00.400
So we like to think of THE DEMATERIALISED

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:03.480
as the digital department
store of your dreams.

00:11:03.480 --> 00:11:06.530
It's not like a regular online shop.

00:11:06.530 --> 00:11:07.760
It's something different.

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:10.560
It's something that takes
you into another space,

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:13.290
another dimension, through
what it looks like basically.

00:11:13.290 --> 00:11:15.506
Say I buy something on DEMATERIALISED

00:11:15.506 --> 00:11:17.272
to wear in the metaverse,

00:11:17.272 --> 00:11:22.250
how do I put it on and show
it off and experience it?

00:11:22.250 --> 00:11:23.440
Well, there are different avenues.

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:24.490
So the first thing is,

00:11:24.490 --> 00:11:28.440
you could wear it on your real
self through an AR filter.

00:11:28.440 --> 00:11:30.140
So that's number one,

00:11:30.140 --> 00:11:32.630
and that's the part where
technology is still,

00:11:32.630 --> 00:11:34.020
you know, a work in progress.

00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:35.970
It's going to get better
and better, we know it.

00:11:35.970 --> 00:11:36.803
But still, you know,

00:11:36.803 --> 00:11:40.162
it definitely won't
look like you right now.

00:11:40.162 --> 00:11:42.477
It will look like you're
digitally enhanced,

00:11:42.477 --> 00:11:43.983
which is a lot of fun.

00:11:43.983 --> 00:11:46.020
We can also do like a bespoke fitting,

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:49.963
in which you get dressed
by a digital tailor.

00:11:51.625 --> 00:11:54.520
Or you could take it into a computer game.

00:11:54.520 --> 00:11:56.410
So right now we have optimized it

00:11:56.410 --> 00:11:57.620
for a couple of computer games,

00:11:57.620 --> 00:12:01.160
and then you can take it
there and wear the new avatar.

00:12:01.160 --> 00:12:03.540
Clothes are a very physical thing.

00:12:03.540 --> 00:12:06.755
So why would anyone wanna
buy a digital dress?

00:12:06.755 --> 00:12:08.000
I think it takes us back

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:09.460
to the dematerialization

00:12:09.460 --> 00:12:10.850
of goods that we've been seeing

00:12:10.850 --> 00:12:12.610
since we have the digital technologies,

00:12:12.610 --> 00:12:14.980
that it comes from the
gaming world, effectively,

00:12:14.980 --> 00:12:18.320
in which your avatar is
part of your personalities.

00:12:18.320 --> 00:12:20.960
So I think as we, regular people,

00:12:20.960 --> 00:12:24.710
start transitioning more and
more into the virtual world,

00:12:24.710 --> 00:12:26.840
spending more time in
virtual environments,

00:12:26.840 --> 00:12:28.990
and that barrier between physical

00:12:28.990 --> 00:12:31.730
and digital starts blurring more and more,

00:12:31.730 --> 00:12:34.420
and we will start
consuming digital fashion,

00:12:34.420 --> 00:12:36.960
digital sneakers, digital makeup,

00:12:36.960 --> 00:12:39.220
digital jewelry as well.

00:12:39.220 --> 00:12:40.460
I mean, I think, effectively,

00:12:40.460 --> 00:12:41.410
it's the biggest revolution

00:12:41.410 --> 00:12:43.516
the fashion industry has seen so far.

00:12:43.516 --> 00:12:45.230
One of the criticisms about digital

00:12:45.230 --> 00:12:46.590
is that it's impersonal

00:12:46.590 --> 00:12:48.540
or people don't think that it's real.

00:12:48.540 --> 00:12:51.800
And we create very emotional experiences.

00:12:51.800 --> 00:12:55.200
The fashion itself looks quite unreal.

00:12:55.200 --> 00:12:57.140
So you could have a dress

00:12:57.140 --> 00:13:00.190
which is on fire, or a dress
which is made of water.

00:13:00.190 --> 00:13:03.670
And that kind of instantly
pulls the user in to say,

00:13:03.670 --> 00:13:04.570
okay, this is different.

00:13:04.570 --> 00:13:07.160
This is different to what
I've seen before on a screen.

00:13:07.160 --> 00:13:11.090
Say I have created a dress,

00:13:11.090 --> 00:13:14.840
and this was printed all
over my digital dress.

00:13:14.840 --> 00:13:15.870
How does someone buy it?

00:13:15.870 --> 00:13:16.910
Can you walk me through it?

00:13:16.910 --> 00:13:19.390
We're working really hard
in making that barrier

00:13:19.390 --> 00:13:22.390
of entry for regular consumers super low.

00:13:22.390 --> 00:13:24.709
Because right now, purchasing an NFT,

00:13:24.709 --> 00:13:28.950
or any crypto asset, per se,
can be a bit complicated.

00:13:28.950 --> 00:13:32.050
And you need to understand
how certain pieces

00:13:32.050 --> 00:13:33.280
of the technology work.

00:13:33.280 --> 00:13:35.420
You need to be able to set up a wallet,

00:13:35.420 --> 00:13:37.390
potentially have Ether in your wallet

00:13:37.390 --> 00:13:39.720
in order to move your NFTs in the future.

00:13:39.720 --> 00:13:42.680
So we're trying to minimize
all of that complexity

00:13:42.680 --> 00:13:43.980
to the bare minimum.

00:13:43.980 --> 00:13:46.395
So they can manage the
ownership in a very simple way

00:13:46.395 --> 00:13:48.310
without having to understand

00:13:48.310 --> 00:13:50.653
how the whole crypto world works.

00:13:55.050 --> 00:13:56.840
Now I'm on my way to meet someone

00:13:56.840 --> 00:14:00.440
who has collaborated closely
with Karinna and Marjorie.

00:14:00.440 --> 00:14:02.470
Benoit Pagotto is one of the brilliant

00:14:02.470 --> 00:14:05.970
new creative entrepreneurs
shaping digital fashion.

00:14:05.970 --> 00:14:07.300
A gamer at heart,

00:14:07.300 --> 00:14:08.610
he's the man behind

00:14:08.610 --> 00:14:12.220
one of the most lucrative
NFT drops to date.

00:14:12.220 --> 00:14:15.270
A fine arts graduate and branding expert,

00:14:15.270 --> 00:14:18.725
Benoit runs RTFKT Studios out of Paris,

00:14:18.725 --> 00:14:21.313
but it was born in the metaverse.

00:14:22.580 --> 00:14:23.632
I was always a gamer

00:14:23.632 --> 00:14:25.384
and always believed that the game world

00:14:25.384 --> 00:14:29.910
was the most innovative and
amazing medium you could have.

00:14:29.910 --> 00:14:31.107
And so that's why, I think,

00:14:31.107 --> 00:14:32.323
we're living in this moment

00:14:32.323 --> 00:14:33.868
where I think I'm very, very lucky,

00:14:33.868 --> 00:14:36.110
because all the stuff I grew up with,

00:14:36.110 --> 00:14:38.140
I can combine all this
knowledge together now

00:14:38.140 --> 00:14:42.130
to make something I truly,
genuinely know how to do it,

00:14:42.130 --> 00:14:42.963
because it's just combining all

00:14:42.963 --> 00:14:44.590
of this stuff I've been learning

00:14:44.590 --> 00:14:46.510
and being passionate about for years.

00:14:46.510 --> 00:14:49.100
So RTFKT is a blueprint
of the brand of the future

00:14:49.100 --> 00:14:50.260
that's evolving in a world

00:14:50.260 --> 00:14:52.870
where gaming is the
main control reference.

00:14:52.870 --> 00:14:57.718
RTFKT, in a way, is
like a digital atelier?

00:14:57.718 --> 00:14:59.320
Yeah, in a sense,

00:14:59.320 --> 00:15:01.520
because when you do 3D,

00:15:01.520 --> 00:15:04.380
and when you do game
assets or digital items,

00:15:04.380 --> 00:15:06.530
there's a lot of
craftsmanship going into it.

00:15:06.530 --> 00:15:09.240
It's a lot of traditional crafts
you can just do virtually.

00:15:09.240 --> 00:15:13.114
So it's sculpting, modeling, texturing.

00:15:13.114 --> 00:15:15.070
So it's a lot of things
you can find as well

00:15:15.070 --> 00:15:16.030
in traditional fashion,

00:15:16.030 --> 00:15:18.914
because have the small hands
"les petites mains", you know?

00:15:18.914 --> 00:15:21.600
So it is, there's a sense

00:15:21.600 --> 00:15:23.550
of craftsmanship in everything we do.

00:15:23.550 --> 00:15:25.752
So it's in a sense, it's
an atelier workshop.

00:15:25.752 --> 00:15:30.752
You sold $3.1 million of virtual sneakers,

00:15:31.152 --> 00:15:33.539
in something like under 10 minutes.

00:15:33.539 --> 00:15:34.736
Yeah. 5 minutes.

00:15:34.736 --> 00:15:36.336
5 minutes.

00:15:36.336 --> 00:15:41.322
Can you walk us through
the process to create that?

00:15:41.322 --> 00:15:43.990
And then how you took it to market?

00:15:43.990 --> 00:15:45.060
We love sneaker culture,

00:15:45.060 --> 00:15:48.480
and we think it's the fashion
item that is the most,

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:51.390
you know, culturally broad,
it's touching everyone.

00:15:51.390 --> 00:15:53.570
You know, whether you're
a kid from the streets,

00:15:53.570 --> 00:15:55.320
or now every high fashion brand is,

00:15:55.320 --> 00:15:57.520
you know, going ham on sneakers well.

00:15:57.520 --> 00:16:01.610
And FEWOCiOUS, I detected him on Twitter,

00:16:01.610 --> 00:16:02.930
and I contacted him and I said,

00:16:02.930 --> 00:16:05.390
hey, like, do you want to make sneakers?

00:16:05.390 --> 00:16:08.040
And he said, it was his
dream since he's a child,

00:16:08.040 --> 00:16:09.840
that one day he would
make a pair of sneakers.

00:16:09.840 --> 00:16:11.300
All the designs were done in a week

00:16:11.300 --> 00:16:12.750
I think total.

00:16:12.750 --> 00:16:14.360
Then we put everything back in 3D

00:16:14.360 --> 00:16:17.203
to show him how it would look
like before making physicals,

00:16:17.203 --> 00:16:18.750
because the goal was always

00:16:18.750 --> 00:16:21.370
to sell the sneakers as digital first,

00:16:21.370 --> 00:16:24.253
but for people to have
later on a tangible reward

00:16:24.253 --> 00:16:27.683
of having the NFT, which
is these shoes actually.

00:16:28.850 --> 00:16:30.160
So that's how it happened.

00:16:30.160 --> 00:16:32.794
Everything was done in
the lapse of two weeks,

00:16:32.794 --> 00:16:36.807
from the design to the
content, to the launch.

00:16:36.807 --> 00:16:39.090
It was really two weeks.

00:16:39.090 --> 00:16:40.930
And we were all surprised
that we never expected

00:16:40.930 --> 00:16:42.547
to make as much.

00:16:42.547 --> 00:16:45.040
And it happened in five minutes,

00:16:45.040 --> 00:16:47.130
so it was funny because
we were just all watching

00:16:47.130 --> 00:16:48.970
the numbers and seeing everyone buying.

00:16:48.970 --> 00:16:51.040
And then we took our calculators and went,

00:16:51.040 --> 00:16:52.490
man, we just made $3 million.

00:16:54.290 --> 00:16:55.460
It was really, everyone was surprised,

00:16:55.460 --> 00:16:57.070
and I think the really great thing

00:16:57.070 --> 00:16:59.267
was that we are a new brand.

00:16:59.267 --> 00:17:04.267
We used digital sneakers first
as a way to launch a product,

00:17:04.430 --> 00:17:08.200
and we did that with an 18-year-old
artist that no one knew.

00:17:08.200 --> 00:17:10.410
And we completely changed
the game of how you can think

00:17:10.410 --> 00:17:13.940
of releasing a fashion item or
releasing a drop of sneakers.

00:17:13.940 --> 00:17:16.020
So that's, I think the
impact is going to be much,

00:17:16.020 --> 00:17:17.800
much bigger than the $3 million.

00:17:17.800 --> 00:17:19.050
The really, really interesting part

00:17:19.050 --> 00:17:20.650
was the process of how we did it,

00:17:20.650 --> 00:17:23.690
and how we managed to completely, I think,

00:17:23.690 --> 00:17:25.360
make history at this time,

00:17:25.360 --> 00:17:27.907
that still today, people are
making it a reference of,

00:17:27.907 --> 00:17:30.870
remember that sneaker drop
that sold out in five minutes

00:17:30.870 --> 00:17:32.100
for $3 million dollars.

00:17:32.100 --> 00:17:33.759
The NFT itself is like a,

00:17:33.759 --> 00:17:37.408
like a subscription service
of exclusive access.

00:17:37.408 --> 00:17:39.020
Only if you have that item,

00:17:39.020 --> 00:17:41.020
you can have access to
these extra experiences

00:17:41.020 --> 00:17:44.459
or products we're going to release.

00:17:44.459 --> 00:17:47.560
RTFKT is at the forefront of luxury NFTs,

00:17:47.560 --> 00:17:51.140
and being a first mover
has its advantages.

00:17:51.140 --> 00:17:54.880
In April, 2021, Benoit and his co-founders

00:17:54.880 --> 00:17:57.120
raised an $8 million funding round

00:17:57.120 --> 00:17:59.250
led by Andreessen Horowitz,

00:17:59.250 --> 00:18:03.120
one of Silicon Valley's most
storied venture capital firms.

00:18:03.120 --> 00:18:06.060
Tell me a little bit about
the reaction from investors.

00:18:06.060 --> 00:18:08.500
Silicon Valley does not have a reputation

00:18:08.500 --> 00:18:10.560
for understanding fashion.

00:18:10.560 --> 00:18:12.110
Nothing in our business is linked

00:18:12.110 --> 00:18:14.060
to the legacy business of fashion.

00:18:14.060 --> 00:18:16.120
And Andreessen Horowitz
have been very active

00:18:16.120 --> 00:18:18.290
in investing in very, very cool new,

00:18:18.290 --> 00:18:19.990
innovative game studios,

00:18:19.990 --> 00:18:23.510
and actually they invested
from the gaming fund, in us.

00:18:23.510 --> 00:18:25.270
So that's why I think
they understood very well

00:18:25.270 --> 00:18:28.010
that the world of gaming
and fashion were merging,

00:18:28.010 --> 00:18:29.570
but that there's very, very few people,

00:18:29.570 --> 00:18:30.860
who can actually put it together

00:18:30.860 --> 00:18:32.410
and make that bridge and make something

00:18:32.410 --> 00:18:35.060
that is culturally
impactful and, you know,

00:18:35.060 --> 00:18:37.950
successful as well, and
finds a product market fit.

00:18:37.950 --> 00:18:40.610
And us we demonstrated
product market fit with,

00:18:40.610 --> 00:18:42.730
you know, millions of dollars.

00:18:42.730 --> 00:18:45.920
You mentioned the FOMO
from the fashion brands.

00:18:45.920 --> 00:18:49.530
When they call you, what
are they asking you about?

00:18:49.530 --> 00:18:51.880
What, you know, what are
they trying to learn?

00:18:51.880 --> 00:18:53.050
How can we learn from you?

00:18:53.050 --> 00:18:54.580
What can we do together?

00:18:54.580 --> 00:18:56.610
Some of them, they were just really trying

00:18:56.610 --> 00:18:58.517
to fish for information for free.

00:18:58.517 --> 00:19:01.145
And we detected that very, very early.

00:19:01.145 --> 00:19:03.660
So culturally, they are not understanding,

00:19:03.660 --> 00:19:05.830
and you can't understand it
from one day to the other.

00:19:05.830 --> 00:19:08.130
So they need to hire the right people.

00:19:08.130 --> 00:19:09.940
I think it's really an HR problem

00:19:09.940 --> 00:19:11.190
they're going to have.

00:19:11.190 --> 00:19:12.823
And second is just that, yeah,

00:19:12.823 --> 00:19:14.390
it's in any case, at best,

00:19:14.390 --> 00:19:16.878
it's going to be their innovation
team taking care of it.

00:19:16.878 --> 00:19:20.370
And honestly, I mean, I
didn't meet all of them,

00:19:20.370 --> 00:19:22.560
but I didn't see innovation coming

00:19:22.560 --> 00:19:24.310
from fashion brands in a long time.

00:19:26.350 --> 00:19:29.560
Virtual fashion is
clearly a space to watch,

00:19:29.560 --> 00:19:33.310
but for now it only
attracts a niche crowd.

00:19:33.310 --> 00:19:35.720
How long will it take
before the full vision

00:19:35.720 --> 00:19:38.860
becomes reality at scale?

00:19:38.860 --> 00:19:40.180
I think it's just the case

00:19:40.180 --> 00:19:42.880
where the storytelling
gets ahead of the reality.

00:19:42.880 --> 00:19:45.610
So this is exactly what
caused the dot com bubble

00:19:45.610 --> 00:19:46.970
in the late 90's, you know.

00:19:46.970 --> 00:19:51.600
And the excitement was real
and it was deserved, you know.

00:19:51.600 --> 00:19:53.630
The internet has changed humanity.

00:19:53.630 --> 00:19:55.160
It just, you know, the storytelling,

00:19:55.160 --> 00:19:57.030
I think got a little bit
ahead of the reality.

00:19:57.030 --> 00:19:58.270
There was more work to do

00:19:58.270 --> 00:20:00.580
before all these things were built up.

00:20:00.580 --> 00:20:02.070
And I think it's the same here, you know?

00:20:02.070 --> 00:20:03.931
People want to kind of
fast forward to the end,

00:20:03.931 --> 00:20:06.155
but A, you can't see the end,

00:20:06.155 --> 00:20:09.780
B, this technology is
still really nascent.

00:20:09.780 --> 00:20:11.960
You know, the way that we
do something like NFTs,

00:20:11.960 --> 00:20:13.270
it's not very mature.

00:20:13.270 --> 00:20:14.610
I think we will see a lot

00:20:14.610 --> 00:20:19.100
of power players emerging from
completely different places,

00:20:19.100 --> 00:20:22.190
de-centralizing the powers
within the fashion industry.

00:20:22.190 --> 00:20:25.430
There might be an amazing
designer in Kenya,

00:20:25.430 --> 00:20:26.990
and is 13-years-old.

00:20:26.990 --> 00:20:29.830
If she or he gets his hands on a computer,

00:20:29.830 --> 00:20:31.170
and can enter the market,

00:20:31.170 --> 00:20:33.750
then we should be there
to allow them to do that.

00:20:33.750 --> 00:20:35.240
The real application is going to be

00:20:35.240 --> 00:20:37.330
once we have augmented reality glasses,

00:20:37.330 --> 00:20:40.560
and you'll be able to see these
virtual garments everywhere,

00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:42.400
and, you know, buy them,

00:20:42.400 --> 00:20:45.700
or see who's got an expensive
NFT-based fashion item.

00:20:45.700 --> 00:20:47.360
That's when augmented reality glasses

00:20:47.360 --> 00:20:48.890
will be mainstream.

00:20:48.890 --> 00:20:50.030
Us, we do already today.

00:20:50.030 --> 00:20:50.900
We just like, you know,

00:20:50.900 --> 00:20:52.397
use your phone with Snapchat filters

00:20:52.397 --> 00:20:54.970
and the technology is still clunky,

00:20:54.970 --> 00:20:57.500
but you can see it's going there for sure.

00:20:57.500 --> 00:20:59.570
That's when it's really going
to start that big shift,

00:20:59.570 --> 00:21:03.100
I think the towards mainly
buying digital items,

00:21:03.100 --> 00:21:04.680
and physical items being more,

00:21:04.680 --> 00:21:06.673
just like very, very practical,

00:21:06.673 --> 00:21:09.957
comfortable stuff that you can wear daily

00:21:09.957 --> 00:21:12.623
when you spend most of your
time in augmented worlds.

00:21:13.700 --> 00:21:17.794
Historically, fashion has
had a poor track record

00:21:17.794 --> 00:21:20.620
in anticipating and
adapting to new technologies

00:21:20.620 --> 00:21:22.450
and platform shifts.

00:21:22.450 --> 00:21:24.000
But this time around,

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:26.570
the big brands seem to be moving faster.

00:21:26.570 --> 00:21:27.750
Whether they can compete

00:21:27.750 --> 00:21:30.822
with the digitally native
entrepreneurs defining the space?

00:21:30.822 --> 00:21:33.693
Well, that remains to be seen.

